  [FAIL] A6-FIXED (f09's own flagged defect, and the repair says f09 MIS-DIAGNOSED IT) f09 attributed 'a large part of the reported scatter' to its own max() branch.  Removing the branch entirely -- replacing it with a prescription that provably has none -- reduces the gap between the two branch groups by only about a tenth.  The branch was not what was driving the split.  What drives it is that f09's 'isolated-branch' objects are simply the ones at the LOWEST internal acceleration, and those are the discrepant ones.  The branch label was a proxy for depth, and depth is what Part 3 has to control for   (f09 prescription: isolated-branch (4 objects) minus external-field-branch (4) = +0.905 dex, perfect sign separation = False.  FIXED: gap +0.801 dex, perfect sign separation = False.  Only 11 per cent of the branch structure was the branch; the rest is a real trend with x_i)

  DECOMPOSING THE THREE f09 ARITHMETIC BUGS ON THE CLASSICAL EIGHT (canonical footing, median dex):
     f09's inputs, fixed prescription                                  +0.293
     + enclosed mass M_b/2 instead of M_b (Wolf+2010)                  +0.590  (+0.297)
     + deprojected r_1/2 = (4/3) R_e instead of R_e                    +0.715  (+0.125)
     + NEWTONIAN external field G M_host/D^2 instead of V_c^2/D        +0.297  (-0.418)
  [FAIL] A7 (AND THIS CHECK FAILS, WHICH IS THE HONEST OUTCOME) I expected the three f09 arithmetic bugs to push the same way.  They do not: two of them (enclosed mass, deprojected radius) made f09's pressure-supported residual too SMALL by +0.42 dex between them, and the third (the true instead of the Newtonian external field) made it too LARGE by a comparable amount.  On the classical eight they very nearly cancel, so f09's classical-dwarf median was accidentally close to right -- and 'nearly cancel on eight objects' is a coincidence of this sample, not a defence of the arithmetic   (+0.293 -> +0.297 dex, net +0.003; the three steps are +0.297, +0.125, -0.418 -- the third has the opposite sign, so they do not compound)

