  median -0.205 dex on N=5  (f09 reported -0.205 on 5 galaxies)
  [FAIL] D2a (I EXPECTED SMALL-N AND I WAS WRONG) drawing five SPARC galaxies at random from all 147 essentially never reproduces f09's -0.205 dex.  Those five really are low.  f09's A3 was not a small-number accident and the deficit at the lowest disc masses is real in the data   (P(median of 5 random SPARC galaxies <= -0.205) = 0.0029 over 20000 draws; the 5-galaxy median has a 16-84 per cent range of -0.047 to +0.074 dex about a whole-sample median of +0.006)
  [PASS] D2b BUT IT IS A PROPERTY OF THE MEASUREMENT, NOT OBVIOUSLY OF THE FRAMEWORK: four of those five galaxies have NO measured V_flat in the SPARC master table at all -- SPARC itself declines to assign them a flat rotation velocity -- and every one of the five has a peak rotation speed within a factor 2.5 of the HI turbulent dispersion (Leroy et al. 2008: 10 +- 2 km/s), where asymmetric drift and the 1/sin(i) inclination error both bite hardest.  Across all 147 galaxies the twenty with the lowest V_max/sigma_HI scatter three times more widely about the kernel than the twenty highest   (4 of 5 have no V_flat; lowest 20 by V_max/sigma_HI: median -0.086, scatter 0.248 (V_max/sigma_HI = 1.8-5.8); highest 20: median +0.088, scatter 0.093)
  ⚠️ THE LIMIT ON D2b, and f09 hit the same wall: V_max/sigma_HI at fixed sigma_HI IS V_max, so SPARC alone cannot
     separate 'the rotation is not a clean tracer' from a plain velocity-scale trend.  f08 already failed on exactly
     this confound.  The defensible statement is narrow: those five galaxies are the least reliable rotation curves
     in SPARC, so they cannot serve as a control -- but the deficit is real in the data and is NOT explained away.
     f09's mass-matched comparison stays unusable, and it is unusable in BOTH directions.
  and the matching itself was apples to oranges: f09 compared dwarf-spheroidal STELLAR mass with SPARC BARYONIC
     mass on gas-dominated dwarf irregulars.  Classical dSph M_b = 3.2e+05-3.9e+07 Msun.

==========================================================================================================================
PART 3.  THE MATCHING, DONE PROPERLY, AND THE RECOMPUTED SEPARATION.
==========================================================================================================================
  f09 took the min-max acceleration span of its eight dwarfs as a 'window'.  With a larger pressure sample that
  span runs from 1e-4 to 30 and swallows all of SPARC, matching nothing.  Instead: every pressure object is
  compared to the SPARC relation AT ITS OWN x_i -- the running median of every SPARC data point within
  0.2 dex -- and objects where SPARC has fewer than 20 points are DROPPED rather than extrapolated to.

