  [FAIL] A9 (FAILS, AND THE FAILURE IS THE SAME STATEMENT AS A1b) the separation was supposed to be robust to my own matching window.  It is not -- it is a MONOTONE FUNCTION OF HOW MUCH EXTRAPOLATION IS ALLOWED, running from 0.9 sigma at strict matching to 2.9 sigma at a 0.40 dex window.  A 0.40 dex window compares an object at x_i = 0.004 with SPARC points at x_i = 0.010, which is a smear and not a match; the significance is bought entirely by re-admitting the deep objects that have no rotating counterpart.  Nothing here reaches three sigma, and the number one quotes is set by the analysis choice rather than by the data   (w=0.15/n=30: +0.067 dex (0.91 sigma, N=13); w=0.20/n=20: +0.064 dex (0.93 sigma, N=14); w=0.25/n=20: +0.071 dex (1.12 sigma, N=16); w=0.30/n=20: +0.097 dex (1.42 sigma, N=19); w=0.30/n=50: +0.088 dex (1.24 sigma, N=14); w=0.40/n=20: +0.205 dex (2.93 sigma, N=22))

==========================================================================================================================
PART 4.  THE SYSTEMATICS THAT COULD ERASE IT, each moved until it does or fails to.
==========================================================================================================================
  Two numbers are tracked through every systematic, because they are different claims:
    RAW      = the median residual of the whole pressure sample against the framework's kernel.  This is the
               liability-table quantity, and it is large.  It is NOT matched to a rotating control.
    MATCHED  = the separation from the rotating control at the SAME internal acceleration.  This is f09's claim,
               and it is the one that just collapsed.

  (a) STELLAR MASS-TO-LIGHT RATIO   Upsilon_V    RAW dex   MATCHED sep   sigma
                                          1.0     +0.781        +0.162    1.62
                                          1.5     +0.613        +0.114    1.51
                                          2.0     +0.489        +0.064    0.93
                                          3.0     +0.322        +0.073    1.02
                                          5.0     +0.154        -0.027   -0.40
  [PASS] S1 the RAW pressure-supported offset is not a stellar mass-to-light ratio: erasing it needs Upsilon_V near 5 for old, metal-poor dwarf-spheroidal populations, roughly three times what anyone publishes (Woo, Courteau & Dekel 2008, MNRAS 390, 1453; Martin, de Jong & Rix 2008, ApJ 684, 1075 use 1-2).  The MATCHED separation, by contrast, is small at every Upsilon and never becomes a result   (RAW: Ups=1.0 +0.781, Ups=2.0 +0.489, Ups=3.0 +0.322, Ups=5.0 +0.154.  MATCHED: Ups=1.0 +0.162 (1.62 sigma), Ups=2.0 +0.064 (0.93 sigma), Ups=3.0 +0.073 (1.02 sigma), Ups=5.0 -0.027 (-0.40 sigma))

  (b) WHICH CORRECT PRESCRIPTION                       RAW dex   MATCHED sep   sigma
                                   f09 max() branch       +0.043        -0.525   -4.06
                                   FM12 eq.60 1-D         +0.694        +0.157    2.08
                                   sphere avg (exact)     +0.489        +0.064    0.93
  [FAIL] S2 (FAILS, AND IT IS THE SAME FAILURE AS P4) the choice among CORRECT external-field prescriptions is not free.  The exact sphere average and the published one-dimensional formula differ by more than 0.1 dex in the RAW offset, so 'treat the external field properly' does not name a number.  The sphere average is used everywhere here because it is exact for an isotropic tracer AND gives the smaller liability -- against interest   (RAW offsets: sphere average +0.489, FM12 eq.60 +0.694, f09 max() +0.043 dex.  Prescription systematic 0.205 dex, about 42 per cent of the raw offset.  Note f09's max() gives the SMALLEST raw offset of the three, so f09 was using the prescription least favourable to its own conclusion)
  [FAIL] S2b (THE SINGLE MOST DECISIVE LINE IN THE FILE) the MATCHED separation is SMALLER THAN ITS OWN PRESCRIPTION SYSTEMATIC.  Between the two defensible external-field treatments the matched separation moves by more than the separation itself; under f09's own max() branch it changes SIGN and lands four sigma on the WRONG side.  A signal smaller than the systematic that moves it is not a measurement, whatever its nominal sigma   (matched separation: sphere average +0.064 dex (0.93 sigma), FM12 eq.60 +0.157 (2.08 sigma), f09 max() -0.525 (-4.06 sigma).  Systematic 0.093 dex against a signal of 0.064 dex -- a ratio of 1.4)

  (c) HOST BARYONIC MASS, scanned by a factor 4 (it sets x_e and hence the whole external-field term):
        host mass x0.5: RAW +0.386 dex, MATCHED separation +0.063 dex (0.91 sigma)
        host mass x1.0: RAW +0.489 dex, MATCHED separation +0.064 dex (0.93 sigma)
        host mass x2.0: RAW +0.618 dex, MATCHED separation +0.070 dex (1.00 sigma)
  [PASS] S3 the RAW offset survives a factor 4 in the assumed host baryonic mass, and cannot be tuned away in the helpful direction: a heavier host means a larger external field, which SUPPRESSES the predicted dispersion and makes the residual LARGER   (x0.5: RAW +0.386, matched +0.063 dex; x1.0: RAW +0.489, matched +0.064 dex; x2.0: RAW +0.618, matched +0.070 dex)

  (d) LEAVE ONE CLASS OUT, canonical footing:
        without classical : N_matched= 10  RAW +0.485  MATCHED +0.064 dex (0.70 sigma, p=0.1690)
        without m31       : N_matched=  8  RAW +0.297  MATCHED +0.112 dex (1.91 sigma, p=0.0337)
        without isolated  : N_matched= 10  RAW +0.556  MATCHED +0.029 dex (0.32 sigma, p=0.5616)
  [PASS] S4 the collapse of the matched separation is not one class's doing: dropping the classical dwarfs, the M31 dwarfs or the isolated dwarfs leaves the matched separation below two sigma every time, while the RAW offset stays large every time.  The two claims come apart in every subsample   (without classical: RAW +0.485, matched +0.064 dex (0.70 sigma, N=10); without m31: RAW +0.297, matched +0.112 dex (1.91 sigma, N=8); without isolated: RAW +0.556, matched +0.029 dex (0.32 sigma, N=10))

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
4e.  OUTER-HALO GLOBULAR CLUSTERS -- pressure-supported, and containing NO dark matter under LCDM either.
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  This is the one class that separates the two readings of the whole pattern.  Under DARK MATTER, globular
  clusters have none, so MOND's boost should make it OVER-predict their dispersions.  Under a reading in which
  PRESSURE SUPPORT itself breaks the kernel, they should behave like the dwarf spheroidals and sit ABOVE it.
  cluster        M_V        L_V   M(Ups=2)      x_i      x_e  sig_obs  sig_pred      dex   source
  Pal 4        -5.83   1.84e+04   3.68e+04   0.0611  0.00825     0.87      2.38   -0.874   Frank et al. 2012 (23 members)
  Pal 14       -5.33   1.16e+04   2.31e+04   0.0127  0.01902     0.38      1.71   -1.306   Jordi et al. 2009 (16 members)
  Pal 3        -5.45   1.29e+04   2.59e+04   0.0267  0.00927     0.80      2.12   -0.844   Baumgardt N-body fit -- MODEL-DEPENDENT
  NGC 2419     -9.42   5.02e+05   1.00e+06   1.0774  0.00971     5.10      6.50   -0.211   Baumgardt N-body fit -- MODEL-DEPENDENT
  [PASS] S5 (AGAINST THE READING THIS WHOLE LINE OF WORK WAS CHASING, AND IT IS THE MOST IMPORTANT RESULT IN THE FILE) at OVERLAPPING internal accelerations the pressure-supported dwarf spheroidals sit ABOVE the framework's kernel and the pressure-supported globular clusters sit far BELOW it.  'Pressure-supported' therefore does NOT predict the sign of the residual.  DARK MATTER does: dwarf spheroidals are dark-matter dominated and globular clusters contain none, so a kernel calibrated on rotation curves must under-predict the first and over-predict the second, which is exactly what is measured.  So even if the matched separation had survived Part 3, the one class that could tell the two explanations apart argues for dark-matter content and not for support type   (globular clusters: Pal 4 -0.874 (x_i=0.061), Pal 14 -1.306 (x_i=0.013), Pal 3 -0.844 (x_i=0.027), NGC 2419 -0.211 (x_i=1.077); median -0.859 dex.  Dwarf spheroidals over the same x_i = 0.01-1.2: median +0.114 dex on N=12.  This reproduces the committed h93 result (sigma_obs/sigma_pred = 0.407 for Pal 4 at Upsilon_V = 1.6, i.e. -0.78 dex) to within the M/L difference.  ⚠️ CAVEAT AGAINST MY OWN POINT: at Upsilon_V = 1 the globular clusters move to -0.558 dex, still strongly negative, and their dispersions rest on 16-23 stars apiece)

==========================================================================================================================
PART 5.  MUTATION CONTROLS.
==========================================================================================================================
  [PASS] M1 with nu = 1 the same quadrature returns the internal Newtonian field EXACTLY at every external field, so the machinery is not manufacturing a coupling   (Newtonian sphere average / x_i = 1.000000000000, 1.000000000000, 1.000000000000)
  [FAIL] M2 (FAILS, AND IT IS THE SAME FAILURE AS A1 -- the mutation control is doing its job) shuffling the rotation/pressure labels between the two MATCHED populations should not reproduce the real separation.  It does, one time in ten.  That is not a broken control; it is the control correctly reporting that there is no separation left to destroy once the acceleration matching is honest.  Note this same shuffle applied to the UNMATCHED samples returns essentially never, which is exactly the point: the unmatched difference is real and the matched one is not   (MATCHED: real 0.064 dex; shuffled median 0.027, 99th percentile 0.107; P(shuffled >= real) = 0.10515.  UNMATCHED, same shuffle: real 0.495 dex, P(shuffled >= real) = 0.00000.  The raw difference is real; the matched one is not)
  [PASS] M3 MUTATION CONTROL: raising a_0 by 100 drives every object deep into the modified regime, the predicted dispersions rise and the residuals must fall.  They do   (median residual +0.489 dex at the canonical a_0, -0.464 at 100 a_0 (-0.952))
  [PASS] M4 SANITY: the rotating control reproduces the published radial-acceleration relation across the whole SPARC sample, not only inside the matched region   (all 147 SPARC galaxies: median +0.006 dex, scatter 0.156)
  [PASS] M5 MUTATION CONTROL on the DATA rather than the theory: inflating every observed dispersion by sqrt(2) must move every residual by exactly +0.301 dex, and nothing else in the pipeline may respond   (median shift +0.301029996 dex against the analytic 2 log10(sqrt(2)) = log10(2) = +0.301029996)

==========================================================================================================================
VERDICT
==========================================================================================================================
  THE 1.73 SIGMA DOES NOT SURVIVE.  WITHDRAW IT.

  Fixing f09's prescription did not kill it; fixing f09's MATCHING did.  f09 called its comparison 'matched
  acceleration', but its window was the min-max span of its own eight dwarfs, 0.0009 to 0.073 in g_bar/a_0, and
  SPARC has essentially no data below about 0.009.  Comparing object by object against the SPARC relation at
  each object's OWN internal acceleration -- and dropping, rather than extrapolating to, the objects where the
  rotating side has no data -- the separation falls from +0.215 dex / 1.73 sigma to +0.064 dex / 0.93 sigma,
  permutation p = 0.103.  It stays under two sigma on both a_0 footings and in every subsample.  Loosening the
  matching window pushes it back up -- 0.9 sigma at 0.15 dex, 1.4 at 0.30, 2.9 at 0.40 -- which is itself the
  finding: the number is a monotone function of how much extrapolation is allowed, and nothing reaches three
  sigma.  A1, A4, A9 and the M2 shuffle all FAIL and are left failing.

  AND THE REASON IS A CONFOUND THAT CANNOT BE FIXED WITH LOCAL GROUP DATA.  Support type and acceleration DEPTH
  are not independent here.  Of the 50 pressure-supported objects, 36 sit BELOW the deepest acceleration SPARC
  reaches.  Those are the discrepant ones: median +0.731 dex, against +0.114 dex for the 14 that overlap the
  rotating sample.  Inside the pressure sample alone the residual runs with log10(x_i) at slope -0.50, r = -0.62.
  'Pressure-supported' and 'three decades below a_0' are the same set of objects in the Local Group, so no
  Local Group comparison can separate them.  That is a structural limit, not a sample-size one, and it applies
  to f09's version of the test as much as to this one.

  WHAT DOES SURVIVE, and it is the liability-table statement rather than f09's:  the RAW offset of the
  pressure-supported Local Group dwarfs against this framework's kernel is large -- classical +0.49 dex, M31
  +0.58 dex, ultra-faints +1.44 dex -- and it survives a factor 3 in Upsilon_V, a factor 4 in host
  mass, and every prescription tried.  What is NOT established is that it has anything to do with pressure
  support rather than with depth.

  THE REST OF THE REPAIR, for the record.

  1. THE PRESCRIPTION IS FIXED, AND f09 MIS-DIAGNOSED ITS OWN DEFECT.  f09's max(isolated, external-field)
     branch is replaced by the exact sphere average of the QUMOND field: no branch, reduces to the framework's
     own kernel when the external field is off, reproduces f01's independently derived nu(x_e)(1+L_e/3) when it
     dominates, and returns the Newtonian field exactly under nu = 1.  But removing the branch entirely changes
     the isolated-versus-external-field gap by only about a tenth: the branch label was a PROXY FOR DEPTH, and
     depth is what the residual actually runs with.  f09's A6 blamed the right symptom on the wrong cause.
     And 'do it properly' does not name a number: the exact sphere average and the published one-dimensional
     formula differ by 0.20 dex in the raw offset, 42 per cent of it (P4 and S2 both FAIL and are left failing).

  2. THREE FURTHER ARITHMETIC BUGS IN f09, and they do NOT all run one way (A7 FAILS).  Total instead of
     enclosed mass (+0.297 dex) and projected instead of deprojected half-light radius (+0.125) both understated
     the liability; the true instead of the Newtonian external field in nu's argument (-0.418) overstated it.
     On the classical eight they nearly cancel, which is a coincidence of that sample and not a defence.

  3. THE ISOLATED DWARFS SAY THE LEAST.  Cetus, Tucana and the other isolated Local Group dwarf spheroidals
     need no external-field prescription at all (x_e ~ 1e-4) and sit +0.117 dex from the kernel, against
     +0.492 for the Milky Way satellites and +0.578 for M31's.  Cetus is +0.047.  The objects where the
     prescription cannot be blamed show the least offset, which points at environment or tides.

  4. THE CLASS THAT WOULD DECIDE THE FORK DECIDES AGAINST IT.  Outer-halo globular clusters are pressure-
     supported and contain no dark matter under LCDM either.  At overlapping internal accelerations the dwarf
     spheroidals sit +0.114 dex ABOVE the kernel and the globular clusters -0.859 dex BELOW it.
     'Pressure-supported' does not predict the sign of the residual.  DARK-MATTER CONTENT does, exactly: dwarf
     spheroidals are dark-matter dominated and globular clusters are not, so a kernel calibrated on rotation
     curves must under-predict the first and over-predict the second.  That is what is measured.  (Reproduces
     the committed h93 result to within the M/L difference; caveat, 16-23 stars per cluster.)

  5. DEFECT 2 IS REAL AND UNRESOLVED.  f09's five-galaxy mass-matched control is NOT small-number noise: five
     random SPARC galaxies reproduce -0.205 dex with p = 0.0029.  Those five are also the least reliable rotation
     curves in SPARC -- 4 of 5 have no measured V_flat at all and every one has V_max within a factor 2.5 of the
     HI turbulent dispersion -- but SPARC alone cannot separate that from a velocity-scale trend, the same
     confound that killed f08.  D2a, D2b and A3 are left FAILING.  The mass-matched comparison is unusable.

  WHAT MAY BE QUOTED AFTER THIS FILE:
   * 'f09's 1.73 sigma rotation-versus-pressure separation is WITHDRAWN.  With object-by-object acceleration
     matching it is +0.064 dex, 0.93 sigma, p = 0.103; it reaches at most 2.9 sigma, and only by
     loosening the matching window to 0.40 dex, which re-admits objects with no rotating counterpart.'
   * 'The pressure-supported Local Group dwarfs DO sit well above this framework's kernel in the raw -- median
     +0.49 dex, robust to a factor 3 in Upsilon_V and a factor 4 in host mass, with a 0.20 dex prescription
     systematic.  That is the liability-table statement and it stands.  What is NOT established is that it has
     anything to do with pressure support rather than with sitting three decades below a_0.'
   * 'Outer-halo globular clusters, equally pressure-supported, sit far BELOW the kernel, so support type does
     not predict the sign; dark-matter content does.'
  Nothing stronger.  That MOND under-predicts dwarf-spheroidal dispersions is long known (Angus 2008, MNRAS 387,
  1481; McGaugh & Milgrom 2013, ApJ 775, 139); what is new here is only the branch-free prescription, the
  honest matching, and the demonstration that the Local Group cannot separate support from depth.

  THE FORK ITSELF IS UNTOUCHED AND STILL OPEN.  Milgrom's theorem -- modified inertia and modified gravity are
  identical for circular orbits and differ for every other orbit (1994; 2011, arXiv:1111.1611) -- is unaffected
  by anything here.  What is gone is the claim that Local Group dwarfs measure it.

